  "faqs": [
    {
      "q": "What is an MCP server?",
      "a": "A program that exposes tools, resources and prompts through the Model Context Protocol so that any MCP-compatible AI application can use them, instead of you building a separate integration for each app."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which language should I use to build an MCP server?",
      "a": "Use the one you know. Official SDKs and tutorials exist for Python, TypeScript, Java, Kotlin, C#, Ruby, Rust and Go. Python and TypeScript have the most beginner material."
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the difference between stdio and Streamable HTTP?",
      "a": "stdio runs the server as a local subprocess of the client and is simplest for learning. Streamable HTTP runs the server as a web service that remote clients reach over HTTP, which needs hosting and authorization."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is an MCP server the same as an API?",
      "a": "No. An MCP server usually wraps an existing API, describing a few operations so a model can choose and call them safely. The API still does the work."
    },
    {
      "q": "How do I test an MCP server?",
      "a": "Use MCP Inspector to list and call your tools directly, then connect the server to a client such as Claude Desktop and try real questions."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is MCP secure?",
      "a": "It can be, if you design for it: least-privilege tools, human approval for actions with side effects, treating fetched content as untrusted, and following the specification's security best practices for remote servers."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can I add an MCP server to my resume?",
      "a": "Yes, if it solves a real problem. Publish the code, a README with an architecture diagram, a few test cases and a note on what went wrong and how you fixed it."
    }
  ],
